#!/bin/sh
# Said here as well as in the control file's Description, because a package
# manager is where a false promise costs most: someone installs, launches,
# gets a dialog they did not expect, and files a bug against a maintainer
# rather than reading a README. The same note ships in
# packaging/aur/cordial-git/cordial-git.install for the same reason.
set -e

case "$1" in
    configure)
        cat <<'MSG'

Cordial ships no Roblox build, and it cannot download one.

Roblox publishes no Android binary of its own -- its endpoint answers
supportsAndroidBinaries: false -- so the client comes from Google Play.
Cordial runs the copy Sober has already unpacked, or an APK you point it at
yourself:

    ~/.var/app/org.vinegarhq.Sober/data/sober/packages/x86_64/com.roblox.client/

Without one, Cordial starts and tells you it found no Roblox build. That is
the whole of what it can do until you supply one.

Start it with `cordial-shell`, or from your application list. `cordial-run`
is the loader the shell launches and is not what you run by hand.

https://github.com/luohoa97/cordial#readme says which claims were measured
and how.

MSG
        ;;
esac

exit 0
